T16.01|A financial model converts assumptions into linked outputs. Good structure separates inputs, calculations and results, uses consistent signs and units, and makes dependencies visible. A model should support a defined decision rather than accumulate calculations without a purpose.~~Hard-coded inputs inside formulas hide assumptions and make review difficult. Document data dates, currencies, units and scenario controls; use checks to distinguish arithmetic integrity from business plausibility.|Input layer: Sources and assumptions.;;Calculation layer: Schedules translating drivers.;;Output layer: Statements, metrics and decisions.|A sales forecast is units × price. Base units are 100 and price ₹10.~~Store 100 and ₹10 in labelled inputs.;;Calculate revenue ₹1,000 through a linked formula.;;Change units to 120 and verify revenue becomes ₹1,200 without editing the formula.~~A transparent dependency makes scenarios reviewable.~~A formula can still be economically wrong if units, net pricing or timing are mismatched.|Define the model purpose.;;Separate inputs from formulas.;;Use consistent units, sources and checks.
T16.02|Historical preparation maps reported statements and notes into consistent model lines. Preserve reconciliations and distinguish reported amounts from analytical adjustments. The history provides driver estimates but should not be treated as an unquestioned forecast template.~~Reclassifications, acquisitions, discontinued operations and currency changes can create false trends. Use comparable definitions and explain missing data or approximations before calculating ratios.|Reported history: Source statement figures.;;Adjusted history: Explicit analytical restatements.;;Driver history: Ratios or operating quantities derived consistently.|Reported costs are ₹80 lakh, including a separately identified ₹10 unusual cost.~~Keep reported cost ₹80 in the source layer.;;Show the supported adjustment −₹10 separately.;;Comparable cost is ₹70; explain whether recurrence or cash consequences invalidate that adjustment.~~A visible bridge preserves auditability.~~Never silently overwrite source data merely to create smoother trends.|Capture source and period.;;Reconcile reclassifications and adjustments.;;Derive drivers only from comparable measures.
T16.03|A three-statement model links earnings, resources and cash. Net income feeds equity; depreciation reduces asset carrying values and is adjusted in indirect cash flow; operating balances connect sales and purchases to settlement; funding schedules connect debt and interest.~~A balance-sheet check is necessary but insufficient. A model can balance through an unjustified cash plug while hiding an impossible financing plan.|Income statement: Performance recognition.;;Balance sheet: Closing resources and claims.;;Cash-flow statement: Dated changes in cash.|Assume an owner contributes ₹100 lakh, equipment costs ₹60 cash and first-year depreciation is ₹12, with no other activity.~~Closing cash is ₹40 and net equipment ₹48.;;The income statement shows ₹12 loss.;;Equity falls to ₹88, matching assets ₹40 + ₹48.~~All three statements reconcile through the same transactions.~~Borrowing, tax and operations add schedules rather than independent unexplained plugs.|Link transactions once across statements.;;Reconcile balances and cash.;;Test business feasibility as well as arithmetic.
T16.04|Operating schedules forecast revenue and cost from quantities, prices, mix and resources. They should reflect contracts, capacity and economic drivers. Applying one percentage to every line can miss fixed-cost leverage, churn, inflation and product shifts.~~Avoid overlapping growth assumptions. If customers and transactions are separately forecast, clarify whether transactions are per customer or total; otherwise growth may be counted twice.|Volume schedule: Customers, units or transactions.;;Pricing schedule: Realised net prices and mix.;;Cost schedule: Variable, fixed and step resource costs.|Forecast 120 units at ₹5 with unit variable cost ₹3 and fixed costs ₹100.~~Revenue = ₹600.;;Variable cost = ₹360.;;Operating result before other items = ₹600 − ₹360 − ₹100 = ₹140.~~The forecast can be traced to concrete operational assumptions.~~Returns, capacity and tax require further inputs rather than being assumed away silently.|Define units and drivers.;;Separate price, mix and volume.;;Validate resources and capacity.
T16.05|Working-capital schedules convert operating activity into closing balances and cash investment. Receivable days link credit sales with collections; inventory days link eligible cost flows with stock; payable days link purchases with supplier funding. Assumptions must reflect seasonality and terms.~~The change in the balance, not its full closing amount, usually enters the working-capital cash bridge. Acquisitions and currency changes may need separate reconciliation.|Receivable schedule: Credit sales and collection timing.;;Inventory schedule: Stock and production needs.;;Payable schedule: Purchases and settlement terms.|Use a simplified 360-day year, annual credit sales ₹360 lakh and receivable days rising from 30 to 40. Assume unchanged sales and no other movements.~~Opening receivables consistent with 30 days are ₹30.;;Closing receivables are ₹40.;;The ₹10 increase uses operating cash.~~A timing assumption can materially change cash without changing sales.~~Real models should use consistent day counts and account for non-cash balance changes.|Match stocks with eligible flows.;;Model opening and closing amounts.;;Separate operating cash changes from other movements.
T16.06|Fixed-asset schedules reconcile opening gross cost, additions, disposals and other changes, then track accumulated depreciation and impairment. Net PPE differs from gross installed investment. Depreciation should reflect asset lives and commissioning dates rather than an arbitrary percentage of sales when detail permits.~~Capital work in progress may not yet produce depreciation or operating capacity. Cash capex can differ from accounting additions because of payables, leases or acquisitions.|Gross asset schedule: Original and added recognised cost.;;Accumulated depreciation: Consumption allocated over time.;;Net carrying amount: Gross value less relevant accumulated reductions.|Opening net PPE is ₹100 lakh, additions ₹20 and depreciation ₹10, with no disposal or other effects.~~Closing net PPE = ₹110.;;Cash investment is not necessarily ₹20 unless additions are all cash-paid.;;Check commissioning dates and existing asset depreciation separately.~~The movement bridge explains the closing asset.~~A sales-based depreciation ratio may drift when capacity is added ahead of revenue.|Separate gross and net values.;;Model lives and commissioning.;;Reconcile additions with cash-flow capex.
T16.07|Debt schedules track opening balances, drawdowns, repayments, rates and fees. Interest depends on balances and timing; cash schedules determine whether borrowing is needed or excess cash can be repaid. Availability and covenants constrain funding.~~Using closing debt for a full year's interest can misstate cost when borrowing changes during the year. Average-balance assumptions are approximations that should be explicit.|Scheduled debt: Contractual maturities and amortisation.;;Revolver: Flexible funding subject to conditions.;;Interest calculation: Balance, rate and timing.|Opening debt is ₹100 lakh. A ₹20 repayment occurs halfway through the year and rate is 10%. Assume simple within-year accrual.~~First-half interest = ₹100 × 10% × 0.5 = ₹5.;;Second-half interest = ₹80 × 10% × 0.5 = ₹4.;;Total interest is ₹9.~~Applying the rate to only closing debt would understate interest by ₹1.~~Fees and floating-rate changes add separate effects.|Map payment dates and resets.;;Check usable funding limits.;;Reconcile interest, debt and cash.
T16.08|Tax schedules translate accounting results into taxable income and cash tax under the chosen jurisdiction and period assumptions. Equity schedules track contributions, profit, distributions and other recognised changes. Deferred tax and cash tax require different calculations.~~A single tax-rate percentage can be inadequate when losses, deduction limits or different tax bases matter. Equity should not be a balancing plug masking unmodelled transactions.|Current tax: Estimated obligation on taxable activity.;;Deferred tax: Qualifying future tax effects.;;Equity roll-forward: Opening equity plus recognised changes.|Opening retained earnings are ₹50 lakh, net income ₹10 and dividends ₹3, with no other changes.~~Closing retained earnings = ₹57.;;The ₹3 dividend is a cash distribution, not an income-statement operating cost.;;Share issuance or OCI would enter separate equity movements if present.~~A clear roll-forward links earnings and ownership balances.~~Tax assumptions need primary jurisdiction-specific verification for actual filing or valuation.|Separate cash and accounting tax.;;Reconcile equity components.;;Label hypothetical rates and legal assumptions.
T16.09|Circularity occurs when formulas depend on their own outputs, often through cash, debt and interest. It should be identified and resolved deliberately rather than hidden through unstable spreadsheet iteration. Model balancing checks expose unexplained resources or claims.~~A circular reference may be economically meaningful, but numerical convergence does not prove correct assumptions. Analytical solutions, lagged conventions or controlled iteration can be used when appropriate and documented.|Intentional circularity: Economic feedback such as funding-dependent interest.;;Accidental circularity: Unintended formula loop.;;Balancing check: Assets minus liabilities and equity.|Assume debt must cover ₹100 lakh deficit plus annual interest at 10% of the same debt.~~Equation is D = 100 + 0.10D.;;Solve 0.90D = 100, giving D ≈ ₹111.11.;;Interest is ₹11.11 and the funding equation reconciles.~~The simplified circularity has an explicit solution.~~Real payment timing or limits can change the equation; uncontrolled iteration may obscure infeasibility.|Locate the dependency loop.;;Choose a justified resolution.;;Test convergence, limits and independent balance checks.
T16.10|Scenario models change coherent groups of assumptions; sensitivity models isolate selected inputs. A controlled model keeps scenario choices central and traceable. Combining the best demand assumption with the worst funding assumption without explanation may create an incoherent case.~~Sensitivity tables show local effects, not probabilities. A scenario's narrative should connect operations, timing and financing, including constraints that formulas might otherwise ignore.|Base scenario: Defined central assumptions.;;Downside or upside: Coherent alternative conditions.;;Sensitivity grid: Selected inputs varied independently.|Base revenue is ₹100 lakh, variable costs 60% and fixed costs ₹30. A downside uses revenue ₹80 and fixed costs ₹35.~~Base EBIT = ₹10.;;Downside EBIT = ₹80 − ₹48 − ₹35 = −₹3.;;Carry the downside into cash, debt and covenants, not just profit.~~Joint assumption changes can create a different funding outcome.~~Assign probabilities only with defensible evidence.|Centralise scenario controls.;;Keep assumptions internally consistent.;;Test statements, valuation and constraints together.
T16.11|DCF models value dated expected cash flows; comparable-company models infer value from selected market multiples and compatible financial measures. They can cross-check one another but rely on different evidence. Model integrity requires claimant consistency and a clear enterprise-equity bridge.~~A multiple should not be applied to mismatched earnings. Equity multiples use equity measures; enterprise multiples generally use compatible pre-financing measures.|DCF: Intrinsic cash-flow model.;;Trading comparables: Market pricing of selected peers.;;Value bridge: Convert operating enterprise value to relevant equity claims.|Assume normalised EBITDA ₹20 lakh and justified illustrative EV/EBITDA multiple 8×, debt ₹40 and excess cash ₹10.~~Enterprise estimate = ₹160.;;Equity estimate = ₹160 − ₹40 + ₹10 = ₹130, before other claims.;;Compare with a coherent DCF and investigate differences.~~Operating value and equity value differ by funding and non-operating items.~~Peer multiple choice is an assumption, not a universal fair-value rule.|Match numerator and denominator.;;Normalise peers and periods.;;Reconcile all non-operating assets and claims.
T16.12|A merger model combines buyer and target operations, transaction financing, purchase accounting, synergies and integration costs. EPS accretion measures accounting per-share change, while NPV assesses value after acquisition price and risk. They are distinct outputs.~~Deal fees, new interest, amortisation, dilution and timing can change earnings. A model must avoid counting synergies in both target value and a separate buyer benefit.|Operating combination: Buyer and target forecasts.;;Financing and consideration: Cash, debt and new shares.;;Purchase accounting: Asset and liability remeasurement effects.|Buyer earns ₹100 lakh on 100 shares. Target adds ₹30 earnings, new interest ₹10 and 20 new shares; ignore other effects.~~Combined earnings = ₹120.;;Combined shares = 120.;;EPS remains ₹1, the original buyer EPS.~~A larger combined company need not create EPS growth.~~Value assessment additionally needs acquisition price, cash flows and integration risk.|Model sources and uses.;;Separate accounting effects from value.;;Reconcile synergies and dilution once.
T16.13|An LBO model funds an acquisition partly with debt and projects cash available for servicing and repayment. Equity return depends on entry price, operating performance, leverage, exit value and holding period. High leverage can magnify both gains and failure risk.~~Debt repayment is a use of cash, not an extra operating profit. An exit multiple is uncertain, and covenant or liquidity failure can occur before a favourable terminal valuation is reached.|Operating cash: Source of service and repayment.;;Debt waterfall: Interest, scheduled repayment and optional paydown.;;Equity exit: Residual after debt and other claims.|Buy enterprise value ₹100 lakh using debt ₹60 and equity ₹40. Assume exit EV ₹120 and remaining debt ₹30, with no other flows or claims.~~Exit equity = ₹120 − ₹30 = ₹90.;;Equity MOIC = ₹90/₹40 = 2.25×.;;Over three years, simplified IRR = 2.25^(1/3) − 1 ≈ 31.04%.~~Operations and debt paydown both affect residual equity.~~Fees, distributions and intermediate funding change actual IRR.|Test debt-service survivability.;;Model cash paydown explicitly.;;Stress exit value, leverage and holding period.
T16.14|Project and property models link construction, operating cash, financing and contractual waterfalls. Timing matters because debt may be drawn before operations start. Property economics use rents, occupancy, expenses and exit assumptions; project finance emphasises contractual cash and service coverage.~~NOI is not automatically equity cash. Capex, taxes, debt service, reserves and fees create further layers. Construction overruns can require new sponsor equity even when the completed asset is attractive.|Construction phase: Investment, drawdowns and delays.;;Operating phase: Revenue, costs and maintenance.;;Funding waterfall: Debt service, reserves and owner distributions.|Property NOI is ₹20 lakh, maintenance capex ₹3 and annual debt service ₹12. Assume no tax or reserve change.~~Cash before debt service = ₹17.;;Simplified equity cash = ₹17 − ₹12 = ₹5.;;A 25% NOI decline leaves ₹15 − ₹3 − ₹12 = zero.~~Equity cash has less downside cushion than headline NOI suggests.~~Actual reserve and covenant definitions must be modelled explicitly.|Separate construction from operations.;;Reconcile NOI to cash.;;Model reserves, service and downside timing.
T16.15|Specialised models reflect business mechanics that generic industrial templates miss. Banks forecast earning assets, funding, credit losses and capital; insurers forecast premiums, claims, reserves and investment results; startups forecast cohorts, burn, funding and dilution.~~Using EBITDA-based industrial cash flows mechanically for a bank or insurer can produce misleading results. Regulatory and contractual constraints often determine distributable equity cash.|Bank model: Spread, losses, funding and capital.;;Insurance model: Underwriting, reserving and assets.;;Startup model: Unit economics, cash runway and ownership.|A startup has ₹30 lakh cash and net monthly burn ₹5, before a new financing round.~~Static runway = 30/5 = 6 months.;;Model hiring, revenue and collections because burn can change.;;Add financing timing and dilution rather than assuming cash appears at the runway endpoint.~~A startup model must show survival and ownership, not just forecast profit.~~A specialised institution also needs its own regulatory and operating logic.|Select the business-specific drivers.;;Model constraints and cash access.;;Avoid importing unsuitable valuation metrics.
T16.16|Model auditing checks formulas, links, units, signs, sources and outputs. Reconciliation tests detect arithmetic problems; reasonableness tests detect implausible business assumptions. Documentation allows another reviewer to reproduce the result without relying on the author's memory.~~A balanced balance sheet does not prove the model is correct. Hard-coded plugs, overwritten formulas and stale source data can preserve totals while damaging decision quality.|Integrity check: Formula and linkage correctness.;;Economic check: Plausible drivers and constraints.;;Reproducibility check: Sources, versions and instructions.|A model forecasts sales ₹100 lakh but receivables ₹200 at unchanged terms.~~Check units and whether a day-count input was entered as a percentage.;;Reconcile receivables with credit sales and collection days.;;Investigate genuine overdue exposure before assuming it is only a formula error.~~Checks should distinguish data errors from unusual economics.~~Document any justified exception instead of disabling the test.|Trace key outputs to inputs.;;Use independent reconciliations.;;Record sources, assumptions and review exceptions.
